<<<
4. The with two nouns & singular verb. Itis the single-handed courage & intrepidity of these men which appeal to the imagination, & are even more marvellous than their adventures.
===
**4.** *The* with two nouns & singular verb. *It is* the single-handed courage & intrepidity *of these men which appeal to the imagination, & are even more marvellous than their adventures.*
>>>

<<<
Two nouns of closely allied meaning are often felt to make no more than a single notion; courage & intrepidity is álmost a hendiadys for intrepid courage; that feeling is here strengthened by the writer's choosing to use only one the instead of two;
===
Two nouns of closely allied meaning are often felt to make no more than a single notion; *courage & intrepidity* is almost a hendiadys for *intrepid courage*; that feeling is here strengthened by the writer's choosing to use only one *the* instead of two;
>>>

<<<
& to ehange appeal & are to appeals & is would be not only legitimate, but an idiomatic improvement.
===
& to change *appeal* & *are* to *appeals* & *is* would be not only legitimate, but an idiomatic improvement.
>>>

<<<
5. Single adverbial the with comparatives. In 'the more the merrier ' we have double the; in ' They are none the better' we have single the, & that is the type here to be discussed;
===
**5.** Single adverbial *the* with comparatives. In 'the more the merrier' we have double *the*; in 'They are none the better' we have single *the*, & that is the type here to be discussed;
>>>

<<<
but in both types the is not the ordinary adjective or é article ', as in ' the table' &c., but an adverb (or, in the double type, two adverbs);
===
but in both types *the* is not the ordinary adjective or 'article', as in 'the table' &c., but an adverb (or, in the double type, two adverbs);
>>>

<<<
the original meanings were in the double type by what (i.e. by how much) & by that (i.e. by so much), & in the single type by that (i.e. thereby or on that account, or sometimes by so much or by that amount).
===
the original meanings were in the double type *by what* (i.e. *by how much*) & *by that* (i.e. *by so much*), & in the single type *by that* (i.e. *thereby* or *on that account*, or sometimes *by so much* or *by that amount*).
>>>

