<<<
the usage here ascribed to the best writers is not universal, but often violated. What is here maintained is that good writers do not, & bad writers do, prefix the to comparatives when it conveys nothing at all;
===
the usage here ascribed to the best writers is not universal, but often violated. What is here maintained is that good writers do not, & bad writers do, prefix *the* to comparatives when it conveys nothing at all;
>>>

<<<
& again that good writers do not, & bad writers do, allow themselves a than after a comparative that has the before it.
===
& again that good writers do not, & bad writers do, allow themselves a *than* after a comparative that has *the* before it.
