<<<
(without the) A, I am no better B, I am no better for taking them C, I am no better than if I had not

taken them D, I am no better than you
===
(without *the*)

	A, I am no better
	B, I am no better for taking them
	C, I am no better than if I had not taken them
	D, I am no better than you
>>>

<<<


(or, with the)

I am none the better

I am none the better for taking

them
===


(or, with *the*)

	*a*, I am none the better
	*b*, I am none the better for taking them
>>>

<<<


I am none the better than if I

had not &c.

I am none the better than you All eight mean the same
===

	*c*, I am none the better than if I had not &c.
	*d*, I am none the better than you

All eight mean the same
>>>

<<<
, but the 'the' forms a, b, c, & d, are idiomatically in order of merit. The the in a means 'thereby °, or more fully 'for taking them', or more fully still 'than if I had not taken them'.
===
, but the 'the' forms *a*, *b*, *c*, & *d*, are idiomatically in order of merit. The *the* in *a* means 'thereby', or more fully 'for taking them', or more fully still 'than if I had not taken them'.
>>>

<<<
So a is better than b & c because it does not say the same thing twice, as they do. And b is better, idiomatically, than c because, though both are tautological, b is at least as often said as a, & is sanctioned by usage, while c is very rare.
===
So *a* is better than *b* & *c* because it does not say the same thing twice, as they do. And *b* is better, idiomatically, than *c* because, though both are tautological, *b* is at least as often said as *a*, & is sanctioned by usage, while *c* is very rare.
>>>

<<<
Yet c, though it is rare, & though nearly everyone who wished to use than would prefer to it the C form without the, is felt to be not worse than clumsy, & less bad at any rate than d, which sounds illogical.
===
Yet *c*, though it is rare, & though nearly everyone who wished to use *than* would prefer to it the C form without *the*, is felt to be not worse than clumsy, & less bad at any rate than *d*, which sounds illogical.
>>>

<<<
It would be hard to prove its illogicality against a defender of it, but there is no difficulty in seeing how the impression comes: 'but I am none the better than you'
===
It would be hard to prove its illogicality against a defender of it, but there is no difficulty in seeing how the impression comes: 'but I am none *the* better than you'
>>>

